# Normalise line endings in the repository to LF, regardless of what the machine
# doing the committing uses.
#
# Not cosmetic here. This tree was written on Windows and is deployed on Linux,
# and it already contains a mix: most source is CRLF, the files added when it was
# prepared for publication are LF. Without this, the first commit from a machine
# with a different core.autocrlf setting rewrites every byte of every file and
# the diff is unreadable.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; binary files are left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Archives are binary and must never be line-ending converted — a WARC is a
# byte-exact record of an HTTP exchange, and rewriting CRLF inside one corrupts
# both the record boundaries and any payload checksum.
*.warc binary
*.warc.gz binary
*.wacz binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
